// K113 — design 275 U4, the `test_suspend` cause in the `main` context. It is a
// FRAME BOUNDARY and NOT an executor obligation, and those are two derived reads
// of one cause set.
//
// `frame_boundary` counts it: design 44 splits a driven body's state machine at
// it, so `driven` below gets a frame and resumes across it. `wraps_main`
// (design 45 item 1's gate) does NOT: the intrinsic codegens to nothing and is
// reached only through an explicit `__saw_drive`, so a `main` whose only
// suspension is one is left an ordinary function — no frame, no entry executor
// wrapped around it.
//
// One class, two answers, and mistaking either for the other has cost a
// landing each way: gating the framing question on the executor one dropped
// every design-44 test's suspension (SL-306's first fix), and gating the
// executor question on the framing one would wrap a plain `main` in a scheduler
// nothing ever cedes to.
//
// `__Frame_main` IS the executor gate's answer, read off the IR: a `main` the
// gate says suspends becomes a frame with a driver wrapped around it (every
// `yield_now`-in-main test in the corpus asserts that frame's presence), and one
// it says does not stays the plain function it was written as.
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-IR-CONTAINS: __Frame_driven
// EXPECT-IR-ABSENT: __Frame_main
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// before
// driven 12
// after

func driven(n: Int) -> Int {
    let bumped = n + 1
    __saw_suspend()
    bumped + 1
}

func main() {
    print("before")
    print("driven {__saw_drive(driven(10))}")
    // The same intrinsic in `main`'s own body: a state boundary the transform
    // would split at inside a driven frame, and no reason at all to give `main`
    // an executor.
    __saw_suspend()
    print("after")
}
